gram from sessions 3 and 4 against its look-fors (built from a child-created algorithm, uses its planned structures, credit line present; E3-PRO-PD-04) and each child's own line naming the suggestion taken and the change made (E3-PRO-PD-05); respond day 21. Tools: a block editor with the block-and-text-view (blocks with icons and short words, event, repeat, if and if-else blocks, a step-through run that highlights each block and shows each variable's value, job: build, trace and debug the program the step-cards planned; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md; our own editor, STANDARD.md "Our own editor", grades 3 to 5 first). Curio Class writes: the worked, shuffled, modify, read, variable and buggy programs with their keys and the exact message the tool shows for each bug, the paper block cards with if and else cards, the feedback look-for form, the trace-table sheet | After unit 2 because g3-make-improve's model_in is the step-card algorithm the children wrote there. In the progression's in-grade order (g3-algorithm-combo notes): make and improve, which introduces the block-and-text-view, before reading a part's purpose, which reads it; then variables and the trace-table; then debugging, which uses the trace-table. Session 1 traces and predicts and session 2 modifies before the make, so no make comes before a trace or modify on the same construct (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 13 to 21, which include the core check weeks 14, 16 and 20, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 4 "Repetition in games" lessons 2, 4, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "modifying a game" before "designing a game" order for sessions 2 and 3, zero added days | The credit line is credit, not blame: a helped part is praised, and feedback forms use the template's kind wording, with no comments on a person. A misspelled variable name is a program bug, never a spelling mark against the child. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Checking data and asking everyone. *Can we trust our data, and how do we collect it so everyone is counted?* Anchor: a class plant table that says one bean plant grew 400 inches in a week | 4 | g3-check-data; g3-attributes; g3-data-groups; g3-ml-new-data | E3-DAT-DC-09; E3-DAT-DI-10; E3-DAT-IM-11; E3-ALG-ML-02 | Concept area: data and analysis, with algorithms (machine learning). Practices: IC3 (say what the data shows and what it cannot); CT7 (patterns across attributes and in the examples); CT6 (plan what to collect and how); ESR1 (who a way of collecting counts or leaves out); HCD10 (include people who answer in different ways); HCD12 (an opt-out box keeps people in control); CT9 (test the sorter again after a change) | Session 1: a paper data-table with planted errors and gaps (a missing cell, a height of 400 inches, a word where a number belongs, a number where a word belongs) is checked for accuracy and completeness; children mark which entries to ask about again and which to leave out, for number and word data both. Retrieval opening: number or word data (grade 2). Session 2 (device in pairs, or one screen): a two-week class weather table with three attributes (temperature, wind, sky) answers a question about how two attributes go together (are windy days colder here?), read from a scaled picture graph (one picture stands for two days) drawn by hand and then made in a graph tool; children write what they found and one thing the data cannot tell them. Session 3: children design a short class data collection on a topic that matters to the class (which plant the class grows in the garden bed in spring, or which book series the class library adds): what to ask, whom to ask (the sources: the class, another class, the school's grown-ups), which tool (a tally chart or a paper survey with picture choices), and how every kind of student can answer (picture choices, home-language words, pointing or saying instead of writing); they compare two plans (asking only the table you sit at, or a picture survey for everyone) and say which groups each would count or leave out; the survey has an opt-out box, collects no names, and children learn they may tell a teacher or a website that they do not want to share. Retrieval opening: which way of asking leaves people out (grade 2). Session 4: the class as a sorter learns from a small training-set of insect and spider picture cards that has few spiders seen from the side, is tested on ten new cards and gets several wrong; more side-view spider examples are added, it is tested again on the same ten cards, and children tally right answers before and after and say why it changed (adding the missing kind helped); a one-screen sorting tool trained by the teacher may show the same. Retrieval opening: which new card will an uneven sorter get wrong (grade 2). Out: no statistics (no mean or median), no large data set, no more than three attributes, no required device for collecting or checking, no ethics of data collection beyond who is counted, no data about named children, no child trains a machine model, no how a model works inside; the word bias may be heard, not required. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 26) stands for it. Tools: a graph tool (enter a small table, show a scaled picture graph or bar graph, nothing stored or sent, job: show the same graph the class drew by hand); optional one-screen picture sorter (trained by the teacher in the room on picture cards, nothing stored or sent, job: show guesses before and after examples are added). Curio Class writes: the plant table with planted errors (invented for practice), the two-week weather table (invented for practice, or the class's own log, see Cross-subject), the graph sheets, the survey planning sheet with an opt-out box, the insect and spider training and test card sets, all drawn by Curio Class | After unit 3 and before the year-end check; after math grade 3's scaled graphs (see Cross-subject). Checking data comes 