## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | How a message travels. *When I type a note, save it and send it to a friend, what parts does it pass through, and what keeps it safe?* Anchor: a note typed on one classroom device turns up on another across the room, and the class maps where it went | 5 | g5-hardware-software; g5-network-parts; g5-cia-triad | E5-SYS-HW-12; E5-SYS-NT-14; E5-SYS-SE-13 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: IC3 (explain how the parts work together); CT6 (break a task into input, processing, storage and output); CT7 (the pattern shared by wired and wireless networks); HCD12 (availability: users can reach their work when they need it); ESR2 (keeping a password and personal information private) | Session 1 (unplugged, then device): students act the parts of a computer (input, processor, memory and storage, output) while a key press becomes a letter on a screen, then trace typing a note and saving it through the four-part system-diagram, saying which part is hardware, which software, and where the note is kept when the device is off; a sensor (a classroom thermometer's reading) is named as another input. Session 2 (unplugged): the parts of a wired and a wireless network labeled on a network-diagram (device, cable, switch, router, access point, modem) and each one's job; the class acts the parts with yarn and traces the note from session 1 to another device, then sorts the parts into wired, wireless or both. Session 3 (unplugged): the cia-triangle, one story a side: a locked diary (only the right people see it), a note changed on the way or a photo that may not be real (it stays correct), a library closed when you need a book (it is there when needed); students match new threats to the side they hurt and say why that side matters, and keep a pretend password private on a paper account. Out: no logic gates, binary or circuits, no protocols, IP addresses or packets (grade 6's g6-packets), no difference between local networks and the internet, no encryption or access control lists, no real account or real school network examined. End-of-unit (day 4): label a new system-diagram and trace a new task through it (E5-SYS-HW-12), sort new network part cards and give each one's job (E5-SYS-NT-14), match four new threats to the triangle's sides with a reason (E5-SYS-SE-13); respond day 5. Tools: any school device with a plain note tool (job: the note typed and saved, session 1). Curio Class writes: role cards for the acted computer and network, the system-diagram and network-diagram mats, the triangle's stories and threat cards, the paper account | First because every later device session stores, sends or protects something: the four-part system-diagram says where unit 3's variables and unit 4's data live, and the triangle names unit 4's data risks. Networks sit before the triangle because the triangle's threats happen on the network-diagram (model_in of g5-cia-triad). Grade 4's three systems rows are fresh, so three sessions meet three ids. Base: archived Year 6 "Communication and collaboration" lesson 6 for session 3's privacy side; sessions 1 and 2 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "communicating responsibly" order (what to share, then why) inside session 3, zero added days | Passwords and personal information: no real password, address or name is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. No student is asked what devices or networks are at home. Yarn lies on the floor, students walk. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | Flowcharts for a game. *How can we draw a game's rules so anyone can follow them, and who is the game for?* Anchor: a step counter's rules (when a step is sensed, add one; when the count reaches the goal, cheer) drawn as a flowchart the class traces together | 5 | g5-visual-algorithm; g5-human-centered | E5-ALG-PS-01; E5-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT6 (say what the game must do); CT7 (a repeat and a variable box reused in each flowchart); CT8 (plan the flowchart before any code); IC3 (a partner traces your flowchart and says what it did); HCD10 (include a user the first design left out); ESR1 (whose values shaped a design, and who it was unfair to) | Session 1 (trace, then modify): a given flowchart for the step counter starts on an event, repeats a step, holds a variable box (count) that changes each time round and a decision diamond that reads it; students trace it on a trace-table, then change the goal in the decision and trace again. Grade 4's numbered sentences become boxes, diamonds and arrows. Session 2 (make): pairs plan, then draw a flowchart for a score game of their own (start event, repeat, score variable, decision on the score) on index cards with branches or in formal symbols, trace it on a trace-table and check it against a partner's trace; every control structure appears, across one or more drawings. Session 3 (society and design): human-centered design in four ideas, ask who will use it, include different users, test with them, improve, and the considerations a designer weighs (user needs, accessibility, fairness, the designer's own values); students find each idea, or its absence, in a familiar given technology (a library catalog search page), say how people made a choice there that was unfair or wrong for some users, fill a benefit-risk-chart, and change one design of their own session 2 game for a player it leaves out. Out: no lists or arrays, no procedures, no more than one level of nested repeat or decision, formal flowchart symbols a help not a demand, no technical detail of how the given technology works, no code yet (unit 3). End-of-unit (day 9): draw a flowchart for a new task with an event, a repeat, a decision and a variable and trace it (E5-ALG-PS-01); for a new given technology, name two human-centered ideas it uses or misses and one change for a user it leaves out, with the reason (E5-ALG-IM-03); respond day 10. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the step-counter flowchart and its trace-table, flowchart index-card sets, the library-catalog design case with screenshots we draw ourselves, the benefit-risk-chart | After unit 1 because the flowchart's variable is a named box in the system-diagram's memory. Before unit 3 because the flowchart is unit 3's plan: session 2's score games become the programs unit 3 builds and its team project assembles, so a design is planned and checked for its users before code (the bar's design before build). Base: archived Year 6 "Variables in games" lesson 4 (designing a game) for session 2, and "Sensing movement" lesson 5 (designing a step counter) for session 1's anchor; session 3 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's design-sheet order (player, rules, score, end) on session 2, zero added days | Session 3 has disability content (a user who cannot see the screen or use a mouse): talk is about what a design lets a person do, never about a classmate, in the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. The step counter is about a toy or a game, never a student's own body, weight or fitness. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 3 | Programs that keep score. *How does a program remember the score, and how do we find what went wrong when it does not?* Anchor: the class's score game from unit 2, built in blocks, that adds a point but never says "you win" | 7 | g5-variables-use; g5-systematic-debug; g5-remix-program; g5-team-project | E5-PRO-VD-06; E5-PRO-TR-08; E5-PRO-PD-04; E5-PRO-PD-05; E5-PRO-RD-07 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT7 (reuse a component); CT8 (plan the build from the flowchart); CT9 (test, debug and refine); HCD11 (test the game with a player who did not write it); IC4 (roles, tasks and a project note); IC5 (driver and navigator switch at the halfway signal); ESR2 (credit the code you reuse) | Session 1 (trace, unplugged first, then device): students act as the computer with labeled paper cups as named boxes for a four-line program, then trace programs that store a value, compare it and change it (a score that goes up, a countdown timer that triggers an event at zero, a typed guess compared with a pretend password); students trace each on a trace-table before it runs, then run it step by step; "set score to score plus 1" is not equality. Session 2 (modify): change the compare value, the update or the start value of a working score game and predict, then check, what changes. Session 3 (debugging as a method): given buggy programs tried with three systematic strategies in turn (trace by hand, test one part at a time, take pieces out to isolate the bug), plus two more the boundary names, reading the tool's message and adding a temporary output (a sound or a shown value) where the bug may be; a test-table records each; students say which strategy found the bug and why. Session 4 (modify): new buggy programs, each pair chooses a strategy, fixes the bug and writes the line "what I tested and what I changed". Session 5 (make, remix): a worked remix is traced first, then pairs modify a working game and combine it with a part of another (a teacher or peer program) to make a new variation (a level, a timer, a new ending), crediting the source in a comment. Session 6 (make, team): teams of three, roles named in advance, build the score game from a unit 2 flowchart: each student builds one component (the score with a variable and a decision, the movement, the timer) and writes an inline comment and a short project note another student could use to put it in place. Session 7 (make, team): components assembled and tested by a student who did not write them, with a test-table; bugs fixed by the session 3 strategies; the team's project note finished (purpose, parts, credits). A rubric with three look-fors gives each team feedback, not a score. Out: no lists, arrays or variable scope, no formula with more than one variable, no version control, no professional debugging tools or automated tests, no generated code, ten lines a program at most. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 18) stands for it. Tools: our own block editor (blocks with a text view and a step-through trace; job: trace, modify and build the programs; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: every worked, buggy and remix program with its text view and tested solution, the trace-tables and test-tables, the paper-cup named-box set, the component templates and project-note sheet, the team rubric | After unit 2 because the programs build its flowcharts and its variable box, and after unit 1 because a variable lives in the system-diagram's memory. Trace before modify before make on each construct: variables (sessions 1, 2), debugging (3, 4), remix and team make (5 to 7). The team project comes last because it uses all four rows. Placed across weeks 11 to 17, which include the core check weeks 11, 16 and 17, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 6 "Variables in games" lessons 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's improve-and-share step folded into session 7's test by a non-author, zero added days | Pair and team roles are named and switch, so no student sits out or holds the keys all session; a team member with a motor or vision disability takes the navigator or tester role by choice, and the editor works by keyboard alone. The pretend password program uses a made-up password, never a real one. Credit is taught as respect for another person's work. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Data that trains a model. *Can we teach a computer to tell our shapes apart, and what should we worry about when tools learn from data?* Anchor: a model trained on the class's first drawings calls every small triangle a circle | 5 | g5-collect-tools; g5-variability; g5-train-model; g5-data-benefits-risks | E5-DAT-DC-09; E5-DAT-DI-10; E5-ALG-ML-02; E5-DAT-IM-11 | Concept area: data and analysis, with machine learning (algorithms and design). Practices: CT8 (plan the training set, then train); CT9 (test the model on new examples and improve the set); CT7 (patterns and outliers in a dataset); IC3 (explain the variability); ESR1 (who gains and who is at risk); ESR2 (protect the people a dataset is about) | Session 1 (device): students collect data of two kinds with a tool, a pretend class survey on a form (which of four indoor games, how many paper-airplane loops) and the classroom thermometer's readings, then organize them in a data-table, label each column numeric or descriptive, sort a numeric column from highest to lowest and group the descriptive answers in the tool. Session 2 (unplugged and device): a given dataset of repeated toy-car ramp times (invented for practice) and the class's own three trials: values are not all the same; students mark the typical values and the outlier on a dot chart, give possible sources (starting the timer late, the car bumping the side, a recording slip, a real change in the ramp) and name one relationship between two columns (ramp height and time). Session 3 (device, make): pairs build a labeled training-set of shapes drawn on the tool's drawing pad (circles, triangles, stars), train a small classifier and test it on new drawings kept in a separate test set, counting how many of ten it got right. Session 4 (device, modify): which drawings are missing from the training-set (small, tilted, thin-lined shapes), add more varied examples, retrain and compare the counts; the class says why training data changes what a model gets right. Session 5 (society): case cards of everyday technologies that use collected data (a weather forecast, a route planner, a step-count tracker, a recommendation list); students fill a benefit-risk-chart (convenience, better predictions; privacy, wrong results, bias), name the cia-triangle side at risk, as review, and give one limit that would make it safer. Out: no mean, median or other statistic computed (math grade 6), no percents (accuracy is a count out of ten), no math of how training works, no making a data tool from scratch, no chart required of the collected data (E5-DAT-DC-09), no technical detail of how a case technology works. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 25) stands for it. Tools: a form and table tool (job: collect and organize, sort and group); a model-training tool with a drawing pad that keeps everything on the device (job: train and test; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the pretend survey, the ramp dataset (invented for practice), the dot chart, the drawing prompts, the pretend-model card set for the paper path, the four case cards with dated source lines | After unit 3 because the table and the model are read with the trace and test habits of unit 3 (the test-table becomes the model's test counts). Collecting and spreading data comes before training because a training-set is data collected and organized; the benefits and risks come last because students have just made a model that was wrong for some shapes. Base: archived Year 6 "Introduction to spreadsheets" lessons 1 and 2 for session 1; sessions 2 to 5 our own | Candidate: kept, the partial base's collecting-then-formatting order on session 1, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no prompt asks about a student's home, body, food, pets or family; the survey is pretend and names no one. No camera or microphone: the model learns from drawings, never faces or voices. Case cards name no company. Ramp: a book-and-board slope on the floor, cars are not thrown. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | Who computing includes, and who decides. *Who were computing tools built for, and when should a person, not a computer, decide?* Anchor: a sign-up form on screen that will not accept a classmate's real-sounding invented name because it has a space and an accent mark | 6 | g5-who-included; g5-use-or-not; g5-professionals | E5-SOC-HI-16; E5-SYS-IM-15; E5-SOC-ET-17; E5-SOC-HU-18; E5-SOC-CE-19 | Concept area: computing and society (with systems and security, how systems change culture and work). Practices: ESR1 (who gains, who is left out); HCD10 (design with the people who use a tool); HCD12 (people kept informed and in control); CT6 (does this problem need computing at all?); IC3 (two views, one dated source each) | Session 1: cases where leaving a group out shaped a tool (a form that does not fit every name, a voice tool that does not hear every accent) and cases where including a group shaped it (captions made with Deaf users, a keyboard for a language made with its speakers, an early programming language written in English-like words so people who were not mathematicians could program), told by what happened with no company named, two views and a dated source each (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days"); students place them on the timeline and list who was included or left out, what it changed, and one change they would make. Session 2: how computing systems change culture and the ways people live and work, helping and hindering (how sharing short videos changed how families keep in touch and how news spreads; how a job such as a translator's or an illustrator's is changing as generative tools arrive, from a dated labor source), without students using those tools. Session 3: when to use computing, from case cards (counting votes in a class election, translating a short sign, choosing who gets the last turn, comforting a friend), with a benefit-risk-chart and one question, should a person decide this?; then why a family might choose to use or not use a new home device (cost, privacy, who in the family can use it), without saying whether a choice is right. Session 4: how professionals in different fields work together with shared digital tools (a wildfire team, firefighters, weather scientists and mapmakers on one live map; a museum exhibit team, an artist, a historian and a programmer on one shared plan), then a short role-play of a class tool's team (designer, programmer, tester, user): what each adds and what goes wrong when one is missing. Out: no independent historical research, no dates or names beyond a major milestone's own, no ethical frameworks, laws or error rates, no use of social media or generative tools, no judgment of a family's choice. End-of-unit (day 31): for a new case, say who was included or left out and what it changed (E5-SOC-HI-16); one way a new system helps and one way it hinders how people live or work (E5-SYS-IM-15); two things a new family weighs about a new device (E5-SOC-ET-17); decide and justify whether a new problem needs computing (E5-SOC-HU-18); how two new professionals share their work with a digital tool (E5-SOC-CE-19); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: unit 2's design for users, unit 3's teams and unit 4's model that was wrong for some shapes become the evidence for who a tool includes and who should decide. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 6, so it can follow the year-end check. Base: archived Year 6 "Communication and collaboration" lessons 3 to 5 for sessions 2 and 4; sessions 1 and 3 our own | Candidate: dropped, an interview with a school worker who uses shared digital tools; case cards carry the id and a visit needs the school's own arrangement, zero days | Session 1 has disability, language and naming content: names on cards are invented, no student's own name is tested on the form, and the language keyboard case is replaced if its community's own account cannot be found (Sensitivity reads). Session 2 touches work and automation: some families' jobs are changing, so no student is asked about a family member's job, and a student's own offer is welcome. Session 3's family case is a pictured family, never a student's own home. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

