No row leans on English language arts or social studies. Writing a project note, explaining a strategy and weighing
two views of a case are practiced here, not owned.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 18 and 25, off the grade 5 core
   check weeks 10, 11, 16, 17, 21, 22 and 29 to 32. docs/courses/CALENDAR.md shows grade 5 week 18 already holding the
   mid-year checks of dance, media arts, theater, visual arts, physical education and ELD, and week 25 media arts' year-end
   check. They stay for the lead's one calendar pass, under the pick in the kindergarten frame's
   For the lead 1. If the pass needs week 18 freed, week 19 or 20 works by moving one unit 3 session ahead of the
   check, and the frame says which; weeks 12 to 15 would need the check after unit 2 and a new unit split.
2. **Year 6 of the partial base.** The pilot maps Year 1 to kindergarten, so Year 6 is mapped here to grade 5. The
   Year 6 lessons were placed from their titles and unit overviews on the archived pages (2026-11-25), not from the
   lesson files; the placement is checked when the store folder is made (pilot For the lead 4). Offered on:
   "Communication and collaboration" lessons 1 and 2 (internet addresses, data packets) to the grade 6 frame
   (g6-packets, MS-SYS-NT-34). The "Sensing movement" unit needs a microcontroller board (STANDARD.md section 5,
   optional); only its step-counter design idea is used, on paper.
3. **The block editor's needs for grade 5 (tool asks).** Our own editor starts with grades 3 to 5 (STANDARD.md "Our
   own editor"). Grade 5 needs from it: variables with set, change and compare blocks; an event block that fires when a
   value is reached; a temporary "show value" or sound block for debugging; comments shown in the text view; and a way
   to paste one saved program's blocks into another (the remix and the team assembly), all in one file with no
   account. A separate small model-training tool with a drawing pad and on-device training is also asked (unit 4);
   until it exists, E5-ALG-ML-02 is met only on the paper path, marked part met. Whether Curio Class builds a training
   tool is a scope call (Cory's); my pick is yes, small and on-device, since grades 4, 5 and 6 rows all lean on one.
4. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the pilot's entries: the archived key stage 2 Year 6 unit pages
   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2), the National Association of the Deaf's captioning pages, the
   W3C Internationalization article "Personal names around the world", and the US Bureau of Labor Statistics
   Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh/) as the dated labor source for unit 5 session 2.
5. **Row changes** (notes and words cells; the lead applies them, listed in the final message): g5-professionals
   gains the boundary's cross-field professionals sharing work through digital tools; g5-variability drops "average"
   for "typical" and says no statistic is computed; g5-collect-tools names numeric and descriptive columns and the
   sort and group in the tool; g5-train-model draws on a drawing pad (no camera or microphone) and adds the look at
   what is missing from the training data; g5-human-centered adds the designer's values and accessibility and
   fairness considerations, and its "human-centred" becomes "human-centered"; g5-variability's "analysed" becomes
   "analyzed".

