250:| 3 | Programs that choose. *How do we turn our game cards into a program that makes choices, and how do we find what goes wrong?* Anchor: the class's own unit 2 game, run on the class screen as blocks, where the character runs into the cone every time because the choice asks the wrong question | 9 | g3-make-improve; g3-read-code; g3-variable-trace; g3-debug-combo | E3-PRO-PD-04; E3-PRO-PD-05; E3-PRO-RD-07; E3-PRO-VD-06; E3-PRO-TR-08 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT8 (build from the plan); ESR2 (credit who wrote the algorithm and who built it); IC5 (take and give feedback, driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal); CT9 (test, debug and refine); IC3 (explain what a part does); CT6 (say what the program must do); CT7 (the variable as a named, reusable value) | Session 1 (device): a worked block program for a game that combines an event (when start is clicked), a sequence, a repeat and a choice (if touching the cone, turn around; else move forward), shown in the block-and-text-view (blocks beside their text, read, not typed), traced and predicted before it runs, then the same program with its blocks shuffled to put back in order. Retrieval opening: what does this event-and-repeat program do (grade 2). Session 2 (modify, device): change the question in the choice, the repeat count or one path, predict, run and compare. Session 3 (make, device): pairs build their own unit 2 algorithm (or a classmate's, with credit) as a block program that uses the structures it planned; the program's first comment line names who wrote the algorithm and who built it. Session 4 (device): pairs trade programs and give feedback with a look-for form (does it do what the plan says, is it clear, one suggestion), take at least one suggestion, change the program, test again and write "what I tested and what I changed"; a pair need not take every suggestion. Session 5 (device or printout): given a short program of ten lines at most in the block-and-text-view, say what one marked part is for in the whole program and what would stop working if it were taken out, tracing by pointing at each step; a worked example is read aloud first, then a partner pair reads a second. Retrieval opening: "First I ___, then I ___" for the session 3 program (grade 2). Session 6 (device or printout): a given game program with a score and a counter; children name each variable, say what is stored in it and what changes it, notice that a variable keeps one name spelled the same every time (a misspelled name makes a second box), and fill a trace-table by hand, one column per variable and one row per step; "score gets score plus 1" is read as take what is in score and put one more into it. Retrieval opening: the named box (grade 2). Session 7 (device): given programs, each with one bug, that together cover all four structures (two blocks in the wrong order, a character that does not move when clicked, a repeat that runs too many times, an if that triggers the wrong action or both paths running); trace with a trace-table where a variable is involved, find the first place it goes wrong, change one thing, test again. Out: no typed text code, no data types, no writing variable code (children identify variables in completed programs), no if inside an if or repeat inside a repeat, no functions, no multilevel project, no formal code review, no reading of error reports, no fixing more than one bug at a time; no single program is required to hold every structure, but the unit's programs hold all four. End-of-unit (day 20): a written sitting with a new short program in the block-and-text-view: say what a marked part is for and what would stop working without it (E3-PRO-RD-07); name its variables, what each stores and what changes it, and fill a four-row trace-table (E3-PRO-VD-06); find and fix the bug in a new program whose if triggers the wrong action and in a second whose repeat runs too many times, on paper (E3-PRO-TR-08); scored beside it, each pair's saved program from sessions 3 and 4 against its look-fors (built from a child-created algorithm, uses its planned structures, credit line present; E3-PRO-PD-04) and each child's own line naming the suggestion taken and the change made (E3-PRO-PD-05); respond day 21. Tools: a block editor with the block-and-text-view (blocks with icons and short words, event, repeat, if and if-else blocks, a step-through run that highlights each block and shows each variable's value, job: build, trace and debug the program the step-cards planned; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md; our own editor, STANDARD.md "Our own editor", grades 3 to 5 first). Curio Class writes: the worked, shuffled, modify, read, variable and buggy programs with their keys and the exact message the tool shows for each bug, the paper block cards with if and else cards, the feedback look-for form, the trace-table sheet | After unit 2 because g3-make-improve's model_in is the step-card algorithm the children wrote there. In the progression's in-grade order (g3-algorithm-combo notes): make and improve, which introduces the block-and-text-view, before reading a part's purpose, which reads it; then variables and the trace-table; then debugging, which uses the trace-table. Session 1 traces and predicts and session 2 modifies before the make, so no make comes before a trace or modify on the same construct (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 13 to 21, which include the core check weeks 14, 16 and 20, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 4 "Repetition in games" lessons 2, 4, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "modifying a game" before "designing a game" order for sessions 2 and 3, zero added days | The credit line is credit, not blame: a helped part is praised, and feedback forms use the template's kind wording, with no comments on a person. A misspelled variable name is a program bug, never a spelling mark against the child. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
251:| 4 | Checking data and asking everyone. *Can we trust our data, and how do we collect it so everyone is counted?* Anchor: a class plant table that says one bean plant grew 400 inches in a week | 4 | g3-check-data; g3-attributes; g3-data-groups; g3-ml-new-data | E3-DAT-DC-09; E3-DAT-DI-10; E3-DAT-IM-11; E3-ALG-ML-02 | Concept area: data and analysis, with algorithms (machine learning). Practices: IC3 (say what the data shows and what it cannot); CT7 (patterns across attributes and in the examples); CT6 (plan what to collect and how); ESR1 (who a way of collecting counts or leaves out); HCD10 (include people who answer in different ways); HCD12 (an opt-out box keeps people in control); CT9 (test the sorter again after a change) | Session 1: a paper data-table with planted errors and gaps (a missing cell, a height of 400 inches, a word where a number belongs, a number where a word belongs) is checked for accuracy and completeness; children mark which entries to ask about again and which to leave out, for number and word data both. Retrieval opening: number or word data (grade 2). Session 2 (device in pairs, or one screen): a two-week class weather table with three attributes (temperature, wind, sky) answers a question about how two attributes go together (are windy days colder here?), read from a scaled picture graph (one picture stands for two days) drawn by hand and then made in a graph tool; children write what they found and one thing the data cannot tell them. Session 3: children design a short class data collection on a topic that matters to the class (which plant the class grows in the garden bed in spring, or which book series the class library adds): what to ask, whom to ask (the sources: the class, another class, the school's grown-ups), which tool (a tally chart or a paper survey with picture choices), and how every kind of student can answer (picture choices, home-language words, pointing or saying instead of writing); they compare two plans (asking only the table you sit at, or a picture survey for everyone) and say which groups each would count or leave out; the survey has an opt-out box, collects no names, and children learn they may tell a teacher or a website that they do not want to share. Retrieval opening: which way of asking leaves people out (grade 2). Session 4: the class as a sorter learns from a small training-set of insect and spider picture cards that has few spiders seen from the side, is tested on ten new cards and gets several wrong; more side-view spider examples are added, it is tested again on the same ten cards, and children tally right answers before and after and say why it changed (adding the missing kind helped); a one-screen sorting tool trained by the teacher may show the same. Retrieval opening: which new card will an uneven sorter get wrong (grade 2). Out: no statistics (no mean or median), no large data set, no more than three attributes, no required device for collecting or checking, no ethics of data collection beyond who is counted, no data about named children, no child trains a machine model, no how a model works inside; the word bias may be heard, not required. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 26) stands for it. Tools: a graph tool (enter a small table, show a scaled picture graph or bar graph, nothing stored or sent, job: show the same graph the class drew by hand); optional one-screen picture sorter (trained by the teacher in the room on picture cards, nothing stored or sent, job: show guesses before and after examples are added). Curio Class writes: the plant table with planted errors (invented for practice), the two-week weather table (invented for practice, or the class's own log, see Cross-subject), the graph sheets, the survey planning sheet with an opt-out box, the insect and spider training and test card sets, all drawn by Curio Class | After unit 3 and before the year-end check; after math grade 3's scaled graphs (see Cross-subject). Checking data comes first so the weather table is trusted before it is read; reading attributes comes before designing a collection, so children know what a good table needs; machine learning closes the unit because its training set is data that can leave a kind out, the same idea as session 3's survey. Base: archived Year 4 "Data logging" lessons 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "data for answers" step (choosing which data answers a question) inside session 2, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home, family or body questions; the survey topics are neutral class choices, and no answer is linked to a named child. A survey everyone can answer: talk stays on the survey's design, never on a classmate's language or ability; the wording follows what the sensitivity read settles from its pack. The sorter's mistakes are about pictures of insects and spiders, never people or faces. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
252:| 5 | Computing at work, then and now. *How do people use computing in their work, and why did people build the tools we use?* Anchor: a picture of a bus driver at the start of a route, a screen beside the wheel, and the question of what the screen helps with | 5 | g3-computing-at-work; g3-why-build | E3-SOC-CE-19; E3-SOC-HI-16; E3-SOC-HU-18 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (who each tool helped and what changed for them); HCD10 (the people each job and tool serves); HCD12 (what a tool lets people do for themselves); IC3 (say what changed and why, with a reason) | Session 1: case cards of workers in different industries (a farmer reading a weather and soil screen, a nurse checking a patient's chart on a computer, a bus driver following a route map, a builder reading plans on a tablet, a shop owner tracking what is left on the shelves), with the computing tool and skill each uses (finding information, storing data, communicating, making plans or pictures); children match worker to tool to skill and say what the job would be like without the tool. Retrieval opening: one interest and one job that uses computing (grade 2). Session 2: one everyday activity at three times on the timeline: finding a fact (a library card drawer, a library computer search, a search on a phone), keeping in touch (a letter, a phone call, a video call), paying at a shop (a written bill, a cash register, a scanned bar code); children say what people do differently now in daily life, work or talking with each other. Retrieval opening: place two tool cards on the timeline (grade 2). Session 3: why people built three computing tools: a weather forecast made on computers (to warn people before a storm), a route finder (to find the way somewhere new), and a voice helper that answers spoken questions (an AI system, built for hands-free help, also for a person who cannot easily see a screen); for each, children write the need or problem it was likely built to meet. The teacher's facts come from the Computer History Museum's online timeline (computerhistory.org/timeline/), the National Weather Service (weather.gov) for forecasts and the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh) for the worker cases, each with its "as of" date in the teacher source line, checked when the cards are written. Out: no how the tools work inside, no skills shown by children, no researching careers in depth or training needed for a job, no timeline of all of computing, no complex effects on society, no comparing tools to pick the best, no named products or companies. End-of-unit (day 31): match two new workers to a tool and a skill and explain one (E3-SOC-CE-19); put three new cards of one activity in order on a timeline and write what people do differently now (E3-SOC-HI-16); for a new pictured tool, write the need it was likely built to meet (E3-SOC-HU-18); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the software and internet of unit 1 seen in people's work and lives, and the algorithms of unit 2 seen as things people built for a need. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 4, so it can follow the year-end check in week 26 and use weeks 28 to 32. The workers and activities do not repeat grade 2's picks (a clinic's records, a tablet drawing, game scores, a song; the abacus, khipu and braille). Our own (the partial base's Year 4 has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses computing for a job; case cards carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | No child is asked about a family member's job; a child's own offer is welcome. The cards show many kinds of work and workers, women and men, rural and town. Older ways of doing things are shown with respect, not as worse; some families still pay with written bills or keep in touch by letter. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
