he table you sit at, or a picture survey for everyone) and say which groups each would count or leave out; the survey has an opt-out box, collects no names, and children learn they may tell a teacher or a website that they do not want to share. Retrieval opening: which way of asking leaves people out (grade 2). Session 4: the class as a sorter learns from a small training-set of insect and spider picture cards that has few spiders seen from the side, is tested on ten new cards and gets several wrong; more side-view spider examples are added, it is tested again on the same ten cards, and children tally right answers before and after and say why it changed (adding the missing kind helped); a one-screen sorting tool trained by the teacher may show the same. Retrieval opening: which new card will an uneven sorter get wrong (grade 2). Out: no statistics (no mean or median), no large data set, no more than three attributes, no required device for collecting or checking, no ethics of data collection beyond who is counted, no data about named children, no child trains a machine model, no how a model works inside; the word bias may be heard, not required. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 26) stands for it. Tools: a graph tool (enter a small table, show a scaled picture graph or bar graph, nothing stored or sent, job: show the same graph the class drew by hand); optional one-screen picture sorter (trained by the teacher in the room on picture cards, nothing stored or sent, job: show guesses before and after examples are added). Curio Class writes: the plant table with planted errors (invented for practice), the two-week weather table (invented for practice, or the class's own log, see Cross-subject), the graph sheets, the survey planning sheet with an opt-out box, the insect and spider training and test card sets, all drawn by Curio Class | After unit 3 and before the year-end check; after math grade 3's scaled graphs (see Cross-subject). Checking data comes 